{"id":"via-francigena-lucca-rome","title":"Via Francigena: Lucca to Rome","who":"Classic route · the medieval pilgrims' road through Tuscany and Lazio","when":"April–June and September–October","vehicle":"On foot, 18–20 days","kind":"walk","color":"#7c2d12","summary":"The Italian end of the old road from Canterbury to Rome: about 410 km through Tuscan hill towns, the towers of San Gimignano, Siena's Campo, the Val d'Orcia, Etruscan sunken roads and Lake Bolsena to St Peter's. The last 100+ km from Viterbo earn the Testimonium certificate.","sources":[{"label":"Camino de Santiago Forum: Via Francigena in May (walkers' notes)","url":"https://www.caminodesantiago.me/community/threads/via-francigena-in-may-26.97200/latest"},{"label":"Cicerone: walking the Via Francigena, part 3","url":"https://www.cicerone.co.uk/walking-the-via-francigena-pilgrim-route-part-3?refer=eavf"}],"approximate":"Usual stage towns; the line follows paths near the waymarked route.","km":430,"bbox":[10.4555,41.9001,12.4575,44.0335],"stops":[{"name":"Lucca","place":"Lucca, Italy","at":[44.0178,10.4544],"text":"Walk the Renaissance walls, then out across the plain.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Italy_-_Lucca_-_2.jpg/500px-Italy_-_Lucca_-_2.jpg","page":"Lucca","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Italy_-_Lucca_-_2.jpg","credit":"Arne Müseler","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0 de"}},{"name":"Altopascio","place":"Altopascio, Italy","at":[43.815,10.6744],"text":"A medieval pilgrims' hospice town whose bell guided walkers in fog.","photo":{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Altopascio_panorama_e_porta.JPG/500px-Altopascio_panorama_e_porta.JPG","page":"Altopascio","source":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Altopascio_panorama_e_porta.JPG","credit":"Serendip at it.wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}},{"name":"San Miniato","place":"San Miniato, Italy","at":[43.6798,10.8501],"text":"A hilltop town famous for white truffles.